Each such operation, $f:D(A)ightarrow A$, is a vector field, where $D(A)$ is an appropriate domain in the algebra's vector space of elements.  In examination of a vector field it is of interest to consider the associated infinitesimal rotation tensor (the exterior derivative of the differential 1-form dual to the vector field; its Hodge dual is the curl of the vector field).  Its computation requires choice of a metric (i.e., a symmetric bilinear form, also referred to as a scalar product or metric tensor).  Specification of the standard topology as above entails imposition of the Euclidean inner product as the metric, allowing the computation to proceed.  However, this choice of metric is arbitrary, and can only be considered a default against which the results of all other choices should be compared.  Thus, with respect to a specific choice of unary operation $f$ to study, we are led to consideration of the mapping,  egin{equation} \slashed{	ext{S}}_f: Sym ightarrow \delta Rot,\end{equation} with,  egin{equation} \slashed{	ext{S}}_f[\mu] = d\left([f(s)]^T \mu \mathbf{d}sight).  \end{equation} In (ef{1/23/25.1}), $Sym$ is the linear space of all possible metric tensors relevant to $\mathbb{R}^n$ (inclusive of metrics that are not necessarily positive definite and not necessarily nondegenerate), and $\delta Rot$ is the linear space of possible infinitesimal rotation tensors relevant \mbox{to $\mathbb{R}^n$}.  Equation (ef{1/23/25.2}) is a formal expression interpreted with respect to the standard basis as follows.  Writing an algebra element as $s=(x_1,\dots,x_n)^T$, we define $\mathbf{d}s \equiv (dx_1,\dots,dx_n)^T$. Thus, $\mu$ is a real symmetric matrix acted upon on the left by the row array $[f(s)]^T$ and on the right by column array $\mathbf{d}s$.  On the right-hand-side of (ef{1/23/25.2}), $d(\cdot)$ represents the exterior derivative operation.  Hence, via the expected symbol manipulations, the right-hand-side of (ef{1/23/25.2}) yields the infinitesimal rotation tensor associated with the vector field $f$ with respect to the metric $\mu$ - or, equivalently, the infinitesimal rotation tensor of vector field $\mu f(s)$ with respect to the Euclidean metric.  $\slashed{	ext{S}}_f$ is a linear transformation taking symmetric tensors to skew-symmetric tensors. Consequently, we call $\slashed{	ext{S}}_f$ the ``Skewer" (the slash through ``symmetric" S represents a skewer, as with a shish-kabob). Since we assume the standard basis, $Sym$ can be represented by a parametrized symmetric $(n	imes n)$-matrix with $ rac{n(n+1)}{2}$ independent parameters, i.e., it is given as a symmetric matrix with a different real parameter in each of its upper triangular entries.  Every possible metric tensor is given by some realization of the set of parameters (i.e., a realization results from any particular choice of $ rac{n(n+1)}{2}$ real values for the parameters).  Consequently, this parametrized symmetric matrix is mapped by the Skewer to a parametrized skew-symmetric matrix representing the resulting infinitesimal rotation tensors.  Each entry not on the main diagonal of the latter matrix is a rational function of the $n$ components of an element as a member of $\mathbb{R}^n$, and the above $ rac{n(n+1)}{2}$ parameters appear as coefficients of constituent rational function terms which sum to the entry.  While the image of the Skewer is thereby complicated, the kernel of the Skewer is another matter.  It is simply a subspace of $Sym$;  the algebra's vector space of elements has vanished.  Thinking globally in the context of this less cluttered environment, we observe that {\it the selected operation $f$ produces a mapping from the set of algebras to the set of subspaces of $Sym$ - whose analysis, for arbitrary $f$, might have much to say regarding algebra structure.} This paper is concerned with establishing the validity of that thesis. Since ``kernel of the Skewer" is a somewhat unwieldy term, we will henceforth instead refer to that entity as the ``anti-rotor" - because each of its members annihilates the infinitesimal rotation tensor.  Results in Section 4 and Section 8 justify beginning our analysis with the algebra's multiplicative inversion operation, i.e., the particular choice $f(s)=s^{-1}$.   egin{definition} With respect to elements $s$ of a given $n$-dimensional algebra $A$ written in the standard basis as $s= (x_1,\dots,x_n)^T$, where these elements are in an open ball in $\mathbb{R}^n$ consisting only of units and whose central point is the multiplicative identity element $\mathbf{1}_A$, an {\it uncurling metric $L$} is a symmetric member of the matrix algebra $M_n(\mathbb{R})$  satisfying,  egin{equation} d\left([Ls^{-1}]\cdot \mathbf{d}sight) = d\left((s^{-1})^T L \mathbf{d}sight) = 0, \end{equation} where $\mathbf{d}s \equiv (dx_1,\dots,dx_n)^T$.  The real vector space of uncurling metrics is the algebra's {\it anti-rotor}, $\mathfrak{u}_A$.  It may also be denoted by $\mathfrak{u}$ if no confusion arises.    \end{definition}   A requisite open ball in the above definition always exists, as can be easily demonstrated by considering the left regular representation of the algebra.  The following simple result indicates the elegant manner in which anti-rotors transform.   egin{theorem}  If $K:A_1ightarrow A_2$ is an algebra isomorphism, then  egin{equation} \mathfrak{u}_{A_1} = K^T \mathfrak{u}_{A_2} K. \end{equation} \end{theorem}   egin{proof} By hypothesis, a nonsingular linear change of variable expression for the elements of $A_2$ in terms of the elements of $A_1$ can be written as $s_2= Ks_1$. \mbox{For $L_2\in \mathfrak{u}_{A_2}$} we obtain,  egin{equation} 0 = d\left((s_2^{-1})^T L_2 \mathbf{d}s_2ight) =  d\left(((Ks_1)^{-1})^T L_2 \mathbf{d}(Ks_1)ight) = d\left((s_1^{-1})^T  ig[K^T L_2K ig]\mathbf{d}s_1ight),   \end{equation} where we have used Definition ef{7/21/23.1}, the commutativity of a differential with a linear transformation, and $(Ks_1)^{-1} = K(s_1^{-1})$ (since $K$ is an isomorphism).   It follows from (ef{9/21/23.1}) that every uncurling metric  of algebra $A_2$ is associated with an uncurling metric of $A_1$, i.e., from the last equality in (ef{9/21/23.1}) we have  $K^TL_2K\in\mathfrak{u}_{A_1}$.  The isomorphism hypothesis allows us to apply an analogous argument employing $K^{-1}$, indicating that for $L_1\in\mathfrak{u}_{A_1}$ we have the associated $(K^{-1})^T L_1 K^{-1} \in \mathfrak{u}_{A_2}$.    Furthermore, $K^T L_2K\in\mathfrak{u}_{A_1}$ implies $(K^{-1})^T [K^T L_2 K]K^{-1} = (K^T)^{-1} [K^T L_2 K]K^{-1} = L_2$.  Similarly, $(K^{-1})^T L_1K^{-1}\in\mathfrak{u}_{A_2}$ is such that $K^T[(K^{-1})^T L_1K^{-1}] K = L_1$.  The theorem follows from the implied bijection. \end{proof}  In other words, recalling the manner in which a metric transforms, Theorem ef{6/6/23.1} says that the anti-rotors of two isomorphic algebras (as two collections of metrics) are the same up to a change of basis.  This is the ``fundamental theorem" of our program, and the rest of this paper is concerned with development of some of its consequences.  But first we observe that computation of $\mathfrak{u}$ is straightforward in principle.  The algorithm is contained in the proof of the following.  Since it will cause no confusion, in the sequel we simply write an algebra element in the standard basis as, e.g., $s=(x_1,\dots,x_n)$, where the latter is understood to be a column vector.   egin{proposition} The members of $\mathfrak{u}$ are the realizations of a symmetric $(n	imes n)$-matrix $M_{\mathfrak{u}}$ whose entries are linear combinations of the components of an $m$-tuple of real parameters, where $m$ is the dimension of $\mathfrak{u}$ as a vector space, and where each realization of $M_{\mathfrak{u}}$ as a real matrix results from replacing its $m$ parameters with the members of any set of $m$ members of $\mathbb{R}$. \end{proposition}   egin{proof} For an $n$-dimensional algebra with elements $s = (x_1,x_2,\dots,x_n)$ with respect to the standard basis, one initially takes $\mathfrak{u}$ to be a symmetric $(n	imes n)$-matrix $M$ whose upper triangular entries are each a different member of a set of $ rac{n(n+1)}{2}$ real parameters.  Computation of $s^{-1}$ produces a vector whose components are each a rational function of the $x_1,x_2,\dots,x_n$.  So far, $Ms^{-1}$ can be understood as a parametrized \mbox{$n$-dimensional} vector field whose components (which can easily be computed) are dependent on the initial set of parameters in $M$ and the $x_1,x_2,\dots,x_n$.  There are then $ rac{n(n-1)}{2}$ constraint equations that must be satisfied in order that (ef{4/7/23.1}) hold (for $L=M$).  Each of these equations has the form,  egin{equation}  rac{\partial}{\partial x_i}([Ms^{-1}]_j) -  rac{\partial}{\partial x_j}([Ms^{-1}]_i) = 0,\end{equation} where $[Ms^{-1}]_k$ indicates the $k$-th component of $Ms^{-1}$.  As noted, we can write $s^{-1} = (\hat{x}_1,\dots,\hat{x}_n)$, where each $\hat{x}_k$ is a rational function of the components of $s$.  Consequently, $[Ms^{-1}]_k$ is a linear combination of rational functions of the components $\hat{x}_1,\dots,\hat{x}_n$, where the coefficients defining this linear combination are linear combinations of various of the above parameters.  It follows that $ rac{\partial}{\partial x_p}([Ms^{-1}]_q)$ is again a sum rational functions of the components $x_1,\dots,x_n$, where this expression is again linear in the parameters.  Thus, multiplying both sides of (ef{9/2/24.2}) by a polynomial determined by the products of the polynomial denominators of the latter rational functions, the left-hand-side of (ef{9/2/24.2}) becomes a polynomial in the components of $s$, while remaining an expression that is linear in the parameters - and the right-hand-side of (ef{9/2/24.2}) remains zero. Hence, satisfaction of each of the $ rac{n(n-1)}{2}$ constraint equations (ef{9/2/24.2}) ultimately reduces to the requirement that a particular multivariate polynomial be {\it identically} zero.  After simplification of the polynomial to be a linear combination of independent monomials, each monomial term is then comprised of a coefficient consisting of a linear combination of some of the above $ rac{n(n+1)}{2}$ parameters, multiplying an expression consisting of a product of some of the components of $s$, each raised to some power.  Therefore, each of the above coefficients of these (independent) monomials are required to be zero.  Accordingly, the number of surviving independent parameters (i.e., parameters not required to be equated to some linear combination of other parameters, or set to 0) is in general reduced as the $ rac{n(n-1)}{2}$ constraint equations are successively treated.  $M$ is then modified as necessary, as each successive constraint equation is treated.  After treatment of all the constraint equations via the above steps defined by treatment of the constraint equations in some sequence, the final number $m$ of surviving independent parameters is the dimension of $\mathfrak{u}$, and the final entries of $M$ evidently yield the requisite $M_\mathfrak{u}$. \end{proof}  Consequently, $\mathfrak{u}$ can be represented as a symmetric $(n	imes n)$-matrix $M_{\mathfrak{u}}$ whose entries are particular linear combinations of $m$ independent parameters (where $m$ is the dimension of $\mathfrak{u}$), because each uncurling metric results from a single set of selected real values for those parameters.  The determinant of $M_{\mathfrak{u}}$, written as ${m det}(M_{\mathfrak{u}})$, is a polynomial in some or all of the components of an ordered $m$-tuple of parameters, $(\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_m)$.  If $m$ is small  we will denote the parameters using different Greek letters like $\alpha$, $ eta$, $\gamma$, etc.  With the above in mind, it is seen that (ef{5/23/23.1}) has immediate consequences - our first family of algebra isomorphism invariants resulting from the anti-rotor.   igskip   egin{corollary} The following are algebra isomorphism invariants.  egin{itemize}  The dimension $m$ of $\mathfrak{u}$.    The largest rank  of a member of $\mathfrak{u}$.   The smallest rank of a nonzero member of $\mathfrak{u}$.  The number of parameters on which ${m det}(M_{\mathfrak{u}})$ depends.  Invariants of the real algebraic set,  egin{equation}V_\mathbb{R}({m det}(M_{\mathfrak{u}})) = \{(\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_m)\in\mathbb{R}^m: {m det}(M_{\mathfrak{u}}) = 0 \}.\end{equation} \end{itemize} \end{corollary}  
ewpage   egin{definition} Let $L$ be an uncurling metric for some algebra $A$.  For $s$ in an open ball centered at the multiplicative identity $\mathbf{1}$ and devoid of non-units, with $\|{ f 1}\|^2 \equiv \mathbf{1}\cdot\mathbf{1}$, the associated {\it unital norm} $ \ell(s)$ is the function  egin{equation}   \ell(s) \equiv \exp\left( rac{1}{\|{ f 1}\|^2}\int_{ f 1}^s [Lt^{-1}]\cdot \mathbf{d}tight).\end{equation} The set of unital norms associated with an algebra, which is an abelian group $\mathfrak{u}^d_A$ with respect to the usual point-wise operation of multiplication of functions, is called the {\it unital norm group}, also known as the {\it dual of the anti-rotor}. $\mathfrak{u}^d_A$ may be denoted as $\mathfrak{u}^d$ if no confusion arises. \end{definition}   The integral in (ef{11/7/20.2}) is path-independent due to (ef{4/7/23.1}).  The duality referred to in Definition ef{9/21/23.2} is easily appreciated.  Equation (ef{9/21/23.2}) can be rewritten as  \mbox{$\log\ell(s) =  rac{1}{\|\mathbf{1}\|^2}\int_{{ f 1}}^{s} \left[L t^{-1}ight]\cdot \mathbf{d}t$.}   We can view this as a forward integral transform, mapping $L$ to $\log \ell(s)$.   The latter equation implies that an uncurling metric and its associated unital norm satisfy,   egin{equation} Ls^{-1}  = 
abla\left[\|{ f 1}\|^2\log  \ell(s)ight] =   \left( rac{\|{ f 1}\|^2}{ \ell(s)}ight)
abla \ell(s).\end{equation}  Due to invertibility of the multiplicative inversion operation,  (ef{11/7/20.1}) can be rewritten as  $Ls = \|\mathbf{1}\|^2 
abla\left[\log \ell(s^{-1})ight]$, where the right-hand-side is interpreted to mean the product of $\|\mathbf{1}\|^2$ with the gradient of $\log \ell$ which is then evaluated at $s^{-1}$.  The latter equation defines the inverse transform - where $L$ (which can be extracted from $Ls$) is obtained from $\log\ell(s)$ (this being an integral transform in the sense of distributions). In other words, groups of uncurling metrics and groups of unital norms are dual (where the duality between individual members of  $\mathfrak{u}_A$ and $\mathfrak{u}_A^d$ follows from an integral transform defined with respect to the multiplicative inversion operation $s\mapsto s^{-1}$).   Also note that according to (ef{11/7/20.1}), the pair $(L, \ell(s))$ characterizes the algebra's multiplicative inversion operation $s\mapsto s^{-1}$ in the portion of an open ball centered at $\mathbf{1}$ devoid of non-units that is orthogonal to the kernel of $L$.  If $L$ is nonsingular, this pair fully characterizes the inversion operation $s^{-1}$ in an open ball centered at $\mathbf{1}$ devoid of non-units.  For a given algebra, the set of all such pairs (where $L$ may or may not be singular) is an abelian group with respect to the binary operation defined by,  egin{equation}  ig(L_1, \ell_1(s) ig) ig(L_2, \ell_2(s) ig) \equiv  ig(L_1+L_2, \ell_1(s)  \ell_2(s) ig).\end{equation}
